# Build output.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash so these match only at the repository root. An
# unanchored `target/` matches a directory of that name at *any* depth, which
# silently swallowed plugin/target/ — the generator's emitters — and left a
# clean checkout unable to build the plugin at all.
#
# Cargo writes to the workspace root only, and the release workflow stages
# binaries in ./dist, so anchoring costs nothing.
/target/
/dist/

# Agent and editor state.
#
# .claude/ is anchored for the same reason the build output is: a directory rule
# without a leading slash matches at any depth, and a source tree is free to
# contain a directory of any name. .DS_Store is left unanchored deliberately —
# Finder writes one into every directory it opens, which is exactly the case a
# depth-matching rule is for.
/.claude/
.DS_Store

# protokit is a separate repository. It was cloned here while its `service`
# package was unreleased; the plugin now depends on the published v1.3.0, so a
# local checkout is only ever for working on protokit itself.
#
# Anchored: only a clone at the repository root is meant, and "protokit" is a
# plausible name for a package directory.
/protokit/

# go.work is committed: every member is inside this repository, so it describes
# the tree rather than one machine, and it is what lets the examples run from the
# repository root. Its checksum file is not — it is a lock over module content
# that go.sum already covers per module.
go.work.sum

# Derived from buf.lock by `just vendor-protos`, purely so the VS Code
# api-linter extension can resolve imports — it reads a path, not a buf module.
# buf is the source of truth; this is a build artifact.
examples/third_party/
